+- **2026-11-13 · Called it on the shown scale: verdicts compare the % a person saw** (rule 1820; amends 1183, 1069, 1415, 1598; lib/how-we-did.ts `verdictFor`, `expectedByStar`, `shownVerdict`, `predictionFor`, `SHOWN_SCALE_FROM`; lib/taste/year-review.ts `callsCard`; app/api/match-csv `predictedVerdict`)
+- **Before → after:** the server's verdict (Spot on / liked it more / less) compared the STORED guess (match_predictions.predicted, rating_predictions.predicted) with the median STORED match of the person's own films at those stars (3+ films, else DEFAULT_BY_STAR), within CALLED_WITHIN 10. The rating sheet (lib/guess-verdict.ts `defaultVerdict`) already compared the SHOWN % (rule 1512's curve), so the two could disagree on one film. Now `verdictFor` takes the stored guess and compares `pctShown(predicted)` with the person's shown median at those stars (`expectedByStar` stores `pctShown(median)`; the curve rises everywhere, so it is the median of the shown numbers), else DEFAULT_BY_STAR read as shown numbers unchanged, within 10. Every server verdict uses it: capture and re-rate (kept in match_predictions.verdict, `verdict_expected` now on the shown scale), the guess check, How was it? (the room's mean), the year in review's calls (`gap` on the shown %), and the new held-out verdict (match-csv `predictedVerdict`). Kept verdicts are not rewritten (rule 1183): one kept before `SHOWN_SCALE_FROM` (2026-11-13T00:00Z) still counts as it is in Called it and Spot on, but the film drawer's line (`predictionFor`) judges that row again on the shown scale. Words: "Spot on" / "We undersold it" (they liked it more) / "We overshot it" (less).
+- **Why:** owner-approved 2026-11-13 (b76-rated-drawer-v2, "Looks good, ship it"): the drawer shows the shown %, so the verdict must read the number on screen, and web, iPhone and the server must agree.
+- **Measured (production, 2026-11-13, SQL on match_predictions, DEFAULT_BY_STAR only, not each person's own table):** 227 rated rows (17 displayed). Spot on 74 → 85, undersold (higher) 117 → 88, overshot (lower) 36 → 54; 57 of 227 change verdict. On the 17 displayed rows Spot on goes 10 → 6. 29 rows carry a kept verdict; 14 of them differ from the new default-table verdict (the drawer re-judges those; the records keep them). Not measured: the per-person tables (they need each library's scores), and whether the shown-scale verdict tracks "liked it" better; that needs the 200+ out-of-sample rows rule 1512 waits for.
+- **Caveat:** DEFAULT_BY_STAR was taken from stored medians (2026-10-09) and is now read as shown numbers without a refit; the shown curve lifts the middle, so a 4★'s typical shown % is likely above 77 and some "We overshot it" at 4★ may be Spot on after a refit. `SHOWN_SCALE_FROM` must be on or after the deploy day: a verdict kept between it and the deploy is on the stored scale but read as shown.
+- **Who it affects / data touched:** every rated film's drawer line and card, the guess line after a rating, Spot on and Called it for ratings from now on, How was it?, the year in review's calls; web, iPhone (reads the server), push copy. No stored data rewritten; new verdicts and `verdict_expected` are written on the shown scale.
+
 - **2026-11-13 · Embedding backfill for watched films; embedding saves logged; the deferred refit waits for the on-demand score** (rule 1840; lib/embed-backfill.ts `runEmbedBackfill`; app/api/cron/ingest-backfill/route.ts; lib/taste/embedding-engine.ts `ensureEmbeddingsForFilms`, `ensureFilmEmbedding`; lib/taste/match-score-update.ts `scoreMissingFilms`)
 - **Before → after:** 2,784 films with more than 1,000 Letterboxd watches (2,782 movies, none duplicates; read 2026-11-13) had no `films.embedding`, so they were never in a nearest-neighbour pool (More like this, similar, the match's anchor and neighbour reads, Pitch's "like X" neighbours, `film_vec`) and a signed-in open embedded them inside the request. Now ingest-backfill's 3-hourly run embeds them, most watched first, 200 a run and 2,000 a UTC day, with the catalog's text recipe (title (year), director, genres, TMDB keywords, synopsis to 400 characters; the 730 with no synopsis and no keywords get the older title, director, genres, nanogenres and tags text, as an on-demand embed already did). Saves now log their error instead of dropping it. On-demand scoring (`scoreMissingFilms`) starts the deferred refit after its own embed and score, not before. Model (text-embedding-3-small), text, thresholds and the match model are unchanged.
